;; Type System Test: laundering a qubit through an UNTYPED binder is CAUGHT
;; EXPECT-MODE: default
;; EXPECT-COMPILE: fail
;; EXPECT-STDERR: linear variable 'q' was consumed more than once
;; EXPECT-STDERR: Refusing to generate code for a program that violates linear

;; This fixture was written as an intentional ACCEPT: the analysis was keyed by
;; NAME, a name was tracked only where the checker could see a linear type on
;; it, and an unannotated parameter erased that -- so `a` was inferred dynamic,
;; the clone inside was not diagnosed, and this program compiled and ran. The
;; comment then standing said the day the hole closed this file must be flipped
;; to a reject DELIBERATELY, by someone who read it, rather than the gap being
;; rediscovered by a consumer. This is that flip.
;;
;; What closed it: `((lambda (a) BODY) q)` is a `let`, and the use analysis now
;; treats it as one. An argument that is a bare reference to a tracked binding
;; is an ALIAS, not a use -- exactly the rule that already caught
;; `(let ((a q)) ...)` -- so the parameter is tracked through BODY and the two
;; uses of `a` are the clone they always were. No type information is needed for
;; this: the alias is a syntactic fact about the application, which is why it
;; can be closed without the full ADR-0004 PlaceId/FlowEnv elaboration.
;;
;; Still NOT closed, and still name-keyed: a qubit laundered through a call to a
;; SEPARATELY DEFINED unannotated function -- `(let ((a (id q))) ...)` where
;; `(define (id x) x)` carries no return annotation. Deciding that needs the
;; return type inferred interprocedurally, which is the remaining half of BI-3.
;; qubit_linear_undecidable_scope_test.esk pins the announced boundary.
(define (h (q : Qubit)) : Qubit q)

(define (laundered (q : Qubit))
  ((lambda (a) (cons (h a) (h a))) q))
